## ABSTRACT
Nanoscale porphyrinic metal-organic frameworks (NMOFs) have emerged as promising therapeutic platforms for the photodynamic therapy (PDT) of cancer in recent years. However, the relative large sizes of current NMOFs ranging from tens to hundreds of nanometers usually lead to inefficient body clearance and unsatisfied PDT effect, thus amplifying their long-term toxicity and restricting their further usages. To overcome these shortcomings, herein, ultrasmall porphyrinic metal-organic framework nanodots (MOF QDs) that prepared from NMOFs are rationally synthesized via a facile method and used as renal-clearable nanoagents for the enhanced PDT of cancer. Compared with the precursor NMOFs, our well-prepared MOF QDs can generate two fold effective toxic reactive oxygen species (ROS) upon the same light irradiation and extremely decrease the inefficacy of the PDT caused by the inefficient use of ROS generated from the interior of NMOFs. Meanwhile, the $\mathrm{IC}_{50}$ value of ultrasmall MOF QDs is nearly one third of that of NMOFs and in vivo results demonstrate that our MOF QDs exhibit better PDT efficacy than NMOFs under the same treatment owing to their overcoming against limited ROS diffusion distance. Significantly, these ultrasmall MOF QDs show efficient tumor accumulation and rapid renal clearance in vivo, indicating their potential in biomedical utility. Last but not least, comprehensive investigations of long-term toxicity of these MOF QDs well demonstrate their overall safety. Therefore, this study will offer valuable insight into the development of safe and high-performance PDT nanoplatforms for further clinical translation.
KEYWORDS: metal-organic framework, nanodots, enhanced photodynamic therapy, renal clearance; long-term toxicity
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted increasing interest in the field of gas storage/separation, catalysis, as well as nonlinear optics due to their distinctive properties including large surface areas, tunable pore sizes, and versatile architectures.1-6 Recently, tremendous effort has been devoted to the development of nanoscale MOFs for biomedical usages.7-11 In particular, by integrating photosensitizers (PSs) in the periodic arrays, nanoscale porphyrinic MOFs (NMOFs) have been well-designed and used as promising therapeutic nanoagents for the photodynamic therapy (PDT) of cancer.12-19 These NMOFs can directly incorporate PSs as efficient building units, providing a high loading capacity of PSs to generate highly cytotoxic reactive oxygen species (ROS), especially singlet oxygen $(^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ , under light irradiation.12, 13 By taking advantages of the relatively isolated PSs in the well-defined structure of NMOFs, aggregation-induced self-quenching effect of PSs can be well avoided.12, 13, 20 Moreover, the porous nature of porphyrinic NMOFs also facilitates the free diffusion of dioxygen and ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ in and out of the frameworks through the channels, therefore increasing the availability of the PSs.21 Though promising, several drawbacks of current NMOF-based PDT systems still remain, and thus restrict their medical usage and further clinical translation. First, NMOFs with a diameter ranges from tens to hundreds of nanometers can be highly uptaken by the reticuloendothelial system (RES) after systemic administration, leading to a post long-term retention in bodies and potential amplification of toxicity.22 Second, ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ generated from the interior of NMOFs with a diameter over $20\mathrm{nm}$ cannot totally diffuse out of the frameworks effectively to process the anticancer function since the ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ will be quenched within $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ of diffusion distance.23-28 Third, the sustained release of unwanted metal ions and toxic organic ligands from those NMOFs with insufficient stability after systemic administration usually induces threatened toxicity in vivo.15, 29-31 Against above battles, it is essential to develop more effective MOF-based platforms with efficient renal clearance, admirable ROS utilization efficacy, and high stability for the PDT of cancer.
It is well known that nanoparticles with a hydrodynamic diameter below $5.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ can easily pass through the kidney filtration and hold renal clearance property. These renal-clearable nanoparticles usually behave like small molecular drugs in both pharmacokinetics and urinary elimination, which thus minimize their non-specific accumulation in RES and potential toxicity.22, 32-38 In addition, it will significantly improve their biosafety whilst these ultrasmall nanoparticles can be renally cleared from the body as their intact form.22 More importantly, these nanoparticles with reasonable sizes and appropriate surface modifications can retain their EPR effect and balance the requirement between clearance efficacy and tumor specificity very well.34, 39 Inspired by aforementioned features, herein we present the rational synthesis of ultrasmall MOF QDs via a facile method, as well as their usage as renal-clearable nanoagents for enhanced PDT of cancer (Figure 1a). By using a liquid phase exfoliation route, these MOF QDs were prepared from zirconium $(Z\mathbf{r})$ -based porphyrinic NMOFs (PCN-224 NMOFs) with high resistance against complicated physiological conditions.15, 40 Due to their ultrasmall size and post polyethylene glycol modification (PEGylation), these well-defined MOF QDs exhibit efficient renal clearance and satisfactory cancer accumulation, overcoming the dilemma associated with long-term toxicity.34 Compared with the precursor NMOFs and PEGylated PCN-224 NMOFs (PEG-MOFs), these MOF QDs can generate two fold effective ROS upon the same light irradiation and provide a better therapeutic efficacy both in vitro and in vivo because their ultrasmall size can facilitate the diffusion of internally generated ROS out of the frameworks by shortening the distance of ROS diffuse inside the frameworks. All these collective properties of MOF QDs not only highlight their promise as renal-clearable nanoagents for enhanced PDT of cancer, but also encourage the further development of ultrasmall MOF-based therapeutic platforms for clinical translation.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Zr-based porphyrinic NMOFs (PCN-224 NMOFs) with a diameter ranging from $50\mathrm{nm}$ to $140\mathrm{nm}$ were firstly prepared as the precursors according to a classical route (Figure S1, Figure S2a, and Figure S2b).15 Upon the treatment of an ultrasonic processor, a liquid phase exfoliation was introduced to yield PCN-224 nanodots (PCN-224 QDs). As shown in Figure S2c, PCN-224 QDs held a mean diameter of $4.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Considering the further bio-related usages of these ultrasmall nanoagents, PCN224 QDs were further modified with polyethylene glycol (PEG) via the strong coordination bonds between $\mathbf{{Zr}_{6}}$ clusters of PCN-224 QDs and terminal carboxyl groups of carboxylated PEG, which were termed as MOF QDs.37, 41 The sizes of MOF QDs were explored by transmission electron microscope (TEM) imaging, atomic force microscope (AFM) imaging, and dynamic light scattering (DLS) measurement.42 TEM images (Figure 1b and Figure S2d) and AFM image (Figure 1c and Figure S3) indicated that these MOF QDs were homogeneous with an average size of $4.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ and a topographic average height of $4.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. Results of DLS measurement demonstrated that these MOF QDs held an average hydrodynamic diameter of $4.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which was below the renal filtration threshold of $5.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 1d). Meanwhile, the hydrodynamic diameter of PCN-224 QDs was smaller than that of MOF QDs, indicating the successful surface modification of carboxylated PEG on PCN-224 QDs. Owing to the larger size of PCN-224 NMOFs, both PCN-224 QDs and MOF QDs held better solubility in water even after $24\mathrm{h}$ of incubation (Figure S4). Figure 1e additionally demonstrated that these MOF QDs could be well dispersed in water, saline $\mathrm{(NaCl,0.9\%)}$ , fetal bovine serum (FBS), and Dulbecco's modified Eagle medium (DMEM). X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns revealed that PCN-224 QDs and MOF QDs held a high degree of consistency with that of pure PCN224, indicating the retaining of crystallinity during the whole synthetic process (Figure 1f). As shown in Figure S5a, thermogravimetric (TG) curves of PCN-224 NMOFs and PCN-224 QDs were quite similar, which indicated the same architecture of PCN-224 NMOFs and PCN-224 QDs. For the first weight loss step, $14\%$ and $8\%$ weight losses were expected for the solvent within the pores for PCN224 NMOFs and PCN-224 QDs $(30-150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . For the second weight loss step, $19\%$ and $20\%$ weight losses were expected for the structural decomposition of PCN-224 NMOFs and PCN-224 QDs (around $490^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ). All these results demonstrated the similar composition of PCN-224 NMOFs and PCN-224 QDs. In addition, TG result shown in Figure S5b indicated that the content of PEG in MOF QDs was about $21\mathrm{wt\%}$ based on the calculated composition. Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) spectra further demonstrated the presence of carboxylated PEG molecules in MOF QDs (Figure S6). Ultraviolet-visible spectroscopy (UV-vis) absorbance spectra indicated that MOF QDs’ chemical structure and related photophysical properties were not affected by the ultrasonic treatment and post PEGylation (Figure 1g and Figure S7). For further bio-related usages, PEG-NMOFs were then prepared with a similar synthetic process and related detail was added in the experimental section. Compared with the positive $\zeta$ potential values of both PCN-224 NMOFs and PCN-224 QDs, the ζ potential values of PEG-NMOFs and MOF QDs exhibited a significant decrease, demonstrating the successful PEGylation of PCN-224 NMOFs and PCN-224 QDs (Figure S8).15 All these results indicated that MOF QDs could be synthesized via a facile yet efficient route with a relatively high productivity.
In order to investigate the formation process of MOF QDs, time-dependent ultrasonic experiment was designed and carried out (Figure 2a and Figure 2b). Compared with the precursors, which defined as pure PCN-224, all the products remained the same crystallinity even when the ultrasonic period was prolonged to 6 h (Figure 2c). However, the sizes of nanoparticles derived from PCN-224 NMOFs decreased step-by-step along with the increase of the ultrasonic period (Figure 2d). Significantly, after 6 h of ultrasonic treatment, most of PCN-224 NMOFs could be transformed into PCN-224 QDs. After careful centrifugation, the uniform PCN-224 QDs were separated from the raw products (Figure 2d). Previous studies demonstrated that the presence of defects in MOFs could naturally affect the amount of adsorbed nitrogen and related experimentally BET surface.43-36 As shown in Figure S9, PCN-224 NMOFs held an experimentally BET surface area of $2316.4~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}.$ , which was $34.6\%$ lower than its theoretical value $(3530.5~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ of defect-free PCN-224 crystals, indicating the presence of abundant defective sites in PCN-224 NMOFs. Therefore, detailed formation process of MOF QDs could be summarized as follows. Firstly, the initial defective sites of PCN-224 NMOFs caused by the locally missed/dislocated ligands and metal ions could significantly decrease the MOF stability.46 Secondly, ultrasonic energy attacked the initial defect sites of PCN-224 NMOFs and small PCN-224 fragments were generated.47, 48 Thirdly, newly exposed surface defective sites on PCN-224 fragments could be further attacked upon sonication and PCN-224 QDs were generated with the increasing of the ultrasonic period.49, 50-55 Significantly, ultrasonic energy only broke the defective sites in NMOFs during the preparation, thus the final PCN-224 QDs still retained the same crystallinity of NMOFs. All these exciting results indicated the rationalization of our design and the successful formation of ultrasmall MOF QDs.
Prior to investigating of the PDT efficacy of MOF QDs, we explored the stability of MOF QDs against various physiological solutions including water, saline, and FBS. Chemical structure information (Figure S10) and concentration-dependent UV-vis absorbance spectra (Figure S11a) of the building units of MOF QDs, tetrakis (4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin (TCPP), as well as related concentration-absorbance calibration curve (Figure S11b) were provided in detail.39 Results of both UV-vis spectroscopy and ICP-OES revealed that all the degradation degrees of MOF QDs in the above physiological solutions were lower than $20\%$ even when the co- incubation period was prolonged to 1 week, indicating the high stability of these MOF QDs (Figure S11c and Figure S11d). The strong interaction between $\mathbf{\Delta}Z\mathbf{r}_{6}$ clusters and carboxylate endowed these MOF QDs with admirable chemical stability.40 Then, DCFH-DA was used as a fluorescent ROS indicator to explore the ROS generating ability of MOF QDs. The fluorescence intensity was plotted against irradiation time upon a $650~\mathrm{nm}$ laser irradiation (Figure 3a). Compared with PCN-224 NMOFs and PEG-NMOFs, PCN-224 QDs and MOF QDs held better ROS generating abilities upon a $650\ \mathrm{nm}$ laser irradiation (Figure S12). Considering ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ as the major ROS generated by porphyrinic MOFs, singlet oxygen sensor green (SOSG) was used as a typical ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ indicator to re-confirm our above results. As shown in Figure S13, both PCN-224 QDs and MOF QDs produced more ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ in the presence of light irradiation than those of PCN-224 NMOFs and PEG-NMOFs. Quantitatively, the generation of ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ was depicted with an exponential function that corresponded to a pseudo first-order process. The ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ generation curve was fitted with the following equation:12
$$
{\mathrm{I}}_{\mathrm{F}}=\mathrm{A}\left(1-\mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{kt}}\right)
$$
$\mathrm{I_{F}}$ was fluorescence intensity and t represented irradiation time, while A and k were fitting parameters (see discussion in experimental section). The product of Ak in the equation was proportional to the initial rate of the reaction that indicated the ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ generating efficacy (Table S1). These results demonstrated that PCN-224 QDs and MOF QDs were at least twice as efficient as PCN-224 NMOFs and PEG-NMOFs in generating ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ upon the same light irradiation, which was attributed to the better use of ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ generated from the interior of PCN-224 QDs and MOF QDs. More importantly, there were negligible differences in the ROS generating ability between PCN-224 QDs and MOF QDs, indicating that PEGylation did not affect the ROS generating ability based on our present design.
Encouraged by the outstanding ROS generating ability of MOF QDs, we further investigated their PDT efficacy in vitro. MTT assays associated with Hela cells without light irradiation revealed that cellular viabilities were not hindered by MOF QDs, demonstrating the negligible cytotoxicity of these MOF QDs (Figure 3b).56-58 Moreover, cellular uptake of MOF QDs was explored. As shown in Figure S14, both MOF QDs and PEG-NMOFs could be effectively internalized by Hela cells. Although the cellular uptake amounts of MOF QDs were lower than those of PEG-NMOFs under the same incubation periods, there were still enough MOF QD-based photosensitizers internalized by Hela cells. By using DCFH-DA as a fluorescent sensor, the ROS generating ability of MOF QDs in Hela cells upon light irradiation could be visualized by a fluorescence microscopy. As shown in Figure 3c, cells in the groups of MOF QDs and light revealed negligible fluorescence, which were similar with that of cells in the control group. However, cells in the group of MOF QDs $^+$ light showed intense green fluorescence, suggesting that a large amount of ROS were generated. Results of flow cytometry reconfirmed that MOF QDs could significantly increase the intracellular ROS levels upon the light irradiation, indicating that our MOF QDs held an excellent light-induced ROS generating ability in vitro. In addition, in vitro PDT efficacy of MOF QDs exhibited a concentration-dependent manner and an irradiation period-dependent manner (Figure 3d and Figure 3e). When the light irradiation intensity and period were defined as 1 W $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ in the typical experiment, the PDT $\mathrm{IC}_{50}$ values of PCN-224 NMOFs, PEG-NMOFs, PCN-224 QDs, and MOF QDs could be calculated as 8.43 μg mL-1, $10.32\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , $3.01\ \upmu\mathrm{g\mL^{-1}}$ , and $3.13~\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{\mL^{-1}}$ , respectively. Owing to the similar structure of PCN-224 QDs and MOF QDs, their $\mathrm{IC}_{50}$ values were almost the same. However, the $\mathrm{IC}_{50}$ value of PCN-224 NMOFs and PEG-NMOFs was much higher than those of PCN-224 QDs and MOF QDs due to the facts including the limited ROS diffusion distance ( $\mathrm{10~nm})$ and the relatively large sizes of PCN-224 NMOFs and PEG-NMOFs. Results of the live-dead cell staining verified the admirable PDT efficacy of MOF QDs (Figure 3f). Analysis based on flow cytometry further revealed that nearly all the cells were killed in the presence of MOF QDs upon the light irradiation via an apoptosis process (Figure 3g and Figure S15).59 All these results demonstrated that our well-prepared MOF QDs exhibited obvious photo-induced cytotoxicity, which could be attributed to the effective use of the internally generated ROS of MOF QDs.
Compared with the size threshold for efficient renal filtration of $5.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ , our well-developed MOF QDs were much smaller, which could extremely avoid the potential long-term retention and toxicity. Accordingly, we further explored the clearance route and bio-distribution of these MOF QDs in detail. Firstly, MOF QDs were intravenously injected into tumor-bearing mice. Secondly, samples including urine, feces, and blood were collected and tested. Time-dependent ICP-OES analysis of the Zr amounts revealed that most of the injected MOF QDs could be found in the urine within 1 week while only a small content was eliminated via the feces, demonstrating that MOF QDs were excreted from the mouse bodies mainly through the renal pathway (Figure 4a). As shown in Figure 4b, MOF QDs could be found in the TEM images of urine samples collected at both $12\mathrm{~h~}$ and $24\mathrm{~h~}$ post-injection (p.i.), indicating that these MOF QDs with high stability against complicated physiological condition could be excreted in their intact form. Time-dependent in vivo and ex vivo fluorescence imaging was then carried out to monitor the bio-distribution of MOF QDs visually. Compared with TCPP, MOF QDs held weaker fluorescence with the same TCPP concentration based on some recent studies including ours (Figure S16a).41 These decrease of fluorescence intensity might stem from the enhanced intersystem crossing (ISC) after coordinating the porphyrin ligands to heavy metal clusters, which was highly consistent with other porphyrinic MOF systems.14 However, above enhanced ISC transition of photosensitizer could extremely improve the ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ yield and enhance the PDT efficacy, which were more suitable for further therapy systems.21 The excitation wavelength of both TCPP and MOF QDs was around $420\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which held low penetration capacity and was not suitable for bio-imaging. Thus, Cy5.5-MOF QDs with an excitation wavelength of $680\mathrm{nm}$ were rationally designed and prepared at first. As shown in Figure S16b, Cy5.5-MOF QDs revealed an excellent fluorescence property with an emission wavelength around $700\mathrm{nm}$ . As shown in Figure 4c and Figure 4d, Cy5.5-MOF QDs could be easily detected in various exposed organs and tumors within the first $12\mathrm{~h~}$ after intravenous injection, which indicated their relatively long blood circulation time and satisfactory tumor accumulation. With the time passing, fluorescent signals from Cy5.5-MOF QDs in various organs decreased sharply, whereas fluorescent signals in tumors altered slightly. These results well demonstrated the efficient renal clearance of MOF QDs, and their high enhanced permeability and retention (EPR) effect in tumors. Blood circulation half-life of MOF QDs could be determined as 2.66 $\pm0.19\mathrm{h}$ , which was slightly shorter than that of PEG-NMOFs $(3.97\pm0.57\mathrm{h})$ , further indicating that MOF QDs with reasonable size and post PEGylation could lead to a relative long blood circulation time (Figure 4e and Figure S17). According to the quantitative analysis of bio-distribution shown in Figure 4f, MOF QDs revealed high accumulation in tumors and the tumor accumulation reached the peak level of $5.21\%\mathrm{ID/g}12\mathrm{h}$ after intravenous injection. Moreover, most of MOF QDs still remained in the kidneys $24\mathrm{~h~}$ after intravenous injection and the levels of $Z\mathbf{r}$ in other organs significantly decreased over time. By contrast, PEG-NMOFs exhibited high accumulation in liver and spleen even at 7 days after intravenous injection, and negligible kidney accumulation was detected during the whole experimental period (Figure S18). Above bio-distribution differences between MOF QDs and PEG-NMOFs indicated that the main clearance pathway of the ultrasmall MOF QDs was through a renal manner. All the above results strongly evidenced the efficient renal clearance of MOF QDs and their potential for further in vivo PDT of cancer.
Figure 5a schematically illustrated the MOF QD-based PDT of cancer and tumor-bearing mice were used to explore the in vivo anti-tumor efficacy of these MOF QDs upon light irradiation. Mice were randomly divided into six groups and defined as control, light alone, MOF QDs alone, MOF QDs $^+$ light, PEG-NMOFs alone, and PEG-NMOFs $+$ light, respectively. Tumor volumes and weights were measured to explore the anti-tumor efficacy of MOF QDs upon light irradiation. As shown in Figure 5b and Figure 5c, mice treated with MOF QDs alone, PEG-NMOFs alone, and light alone showed no obvious effect on the tumor growth as compared with the control group. By contrast, groups including MOF QDs $^+$ light and PEG-NMOFs $^+$ light held obvious suppressive effects on tumor growth. More importantly, complete tumor elimination occurred in the group of MOF QDs $^+$ light at 18 days postinitiation of the treatment, and no tumor relapse was observed in further days, indicating a more significant tumor growth-inhibition effect compared with PEG-NMOFs $^+$ light. In order to obtain accurate and intact staining information of tumors, 14 days were selected as the experimental terminal point to perform H&E staining and TENEL staining for further exploration of therapeutic efficacy.60 As expected, group of MOF QDs $^+$ light held the highest level of tumor cell apoptosis among all the experimental groups (Figure 5d). Moreover, mice in the groups of MOF QDs $^+$ light significantly prolonged survival life time compared to other groups (Figure S19). According to above results, MOF QDs held admirable PDT efficacy for cancer therapy. Given their excellent therapeutic efficacy, we then investigated the long-term toxicity of these MOF QDs in healthy mice after intravenous injection.61 Compared with the control group, mice in the test group showed negligible differences in body weight, eating, drinking, and activity during the whole experimental period (Figure 5e). Hemolytic assay was carried out to explore the interaction between our MOF QDs and blood components. No hemolysis could be found after the co-incubation with MOF QDs (Figure S20). H&E staining images of major exposed organs from mice after different treatments indicated that MOF QDs held high biocompatibility and negligible systemic toxicity (Figure 5f). To provide a quantitative assessment of long-term toxicity, results of blood biochemistry and hematology revealed that there were no significant differences between the test group and the control group, and all the parameters fell in the reference index (Figure S21). All these results suggested the overall safety of our MOF QDs and promised their potential for further practical therapeutic usages.
## CONCLUSIONS
In summary, we rationally designed and synthesized ultrasmall MOF QDs via a facile yet efficient approach, and utilized them as high-performance renal-clearable nanoagents for the enhanced PDT of cancer. These MOF QDs with well-defined crystal structure held efficient renal clearance and sufficient tumor accumulation, which could extremely overcome the long-term toxicity induced by the undefined residue after systemic administration. Compared with NMOFs and PEG-NMOF, our MOF QDs can generate two fold effective ROS upon the same light irradiation conditions and extremely decrease the inefficacy of the PDT caused by the inefficient use of ROS generated from the interior of NMOFs and PEG-NMOFs. Moreover, long-term toxicity after intravenous injection of these MOF QDs indicated their extremely low systemic toxicity and overall safety. We believed that this study not only facilitated the utilization of ultrasmall renal-clearable MOF QDs for non-toxic and efficient cancer therapy, but also encouraged the development of versatile MOF-based nanoplatforms for further biomedical utility.
## METHODS
Chemicals. Tetrakis (4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin (TCPP) and benzoic acid (BA) were purchased from TCI. Zirconyl chloride octahydrate $\mathrm{(ZrOCl_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O)}$ , DCFH-DA, calcein AM, and propidium iodide (PI) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. HOOC-PEG-COOH $(\mathrm{M}_{\mathrm{W}}=1000^{\circ}$ and Cy5.5-PEG-COOH $(\mathrm{MW}=1000)$ ) were purchased from ToYong Biotechnology. N, N-Dimethyformamide (DMF) was purchased from Aladdin. Singlet oxygen sensor green (SOSG) reagent was purchased from Thermo Fisher Scientific. Ltd. Ultrapure water was prepared via a Milli-Q-Plus water system $(18.2\mathrm{M}\Omega\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ ) and used in all the experiments. Dialysis bags (molecular weight cut off: $3.5\mathrm{kDa}$ ) were ordered from Spectrumlabs (U.S.A.). Other reagents were of analytical grade and used as received.
Synthesis of PCN-224 NMOFs. TCPP ( $\mathrm{100~mg)}$ , ${\mathrm{ZrOCl}}_{2}{\cdot}8{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{300~mg)}$ , and BA $(2.8\ \mathrm{g})$ were dissolved in DMF ( $100~\mathrm{mL}$ ) and the mixture was stirred at $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . 5 h later, PCN-224 NMOFs were collected by centrifugation ( $18000~\mathrm{rpm}$ , $30~\mathrm{min}\dot{}$ ), washed with DMF, and re-dispersed in DMF for further use.
Synthesis of PEG-NMOFs. PCN-224 NMOFs $\left(10\mathrm{mg}\right)$ were mixed with excessive HOOC-PEG-COOH $\left(50\mathrm{mg}\right)$ in ultrapure water under stirring at room temperature. 30 min later, the obtained PEG-NMOFs were collected by centrifugation and freeze-dried for further use. Synthesis of PCN-224 QDs. Firstly, PCN-224 NMOFs ( $\mathrm{100~mg)}$ were dissolved in ultrapure water $(100~\mathrm{mL})$ at room temperature. Secondly, the obtained suspension was treated with an ultrasonic processor at a power of 200 watts. After centrifugation ( $12000~\mathrm{rpm}$ , $20~\mathrm{min}$ ), PCN-224 QDs were collected in supernatant, washed with ultrapure water via dialysis, and lyophilized for further use.
Synthesis of MOF QDs. PCN-224 QDs ( $\mathrm{\Delta}\cdot\mathrm{\Delta}10\ \mathrm{mg})$ in ultrapure water ( $\mathrm{i}0\mathrm{mL}$ were mixed with HOOCPEG-COOH $\mathrm{50~mg)}$ under stirring at room temperature. $30\mathrm{min}$ later, above solution was purified via dialysis and lyophilized to result in MOF QDs.   
Synthesis of Cy5.5-MOF QDs. PCN-224 QDs ( $\mathrm{~\mathcal~{~l~}~m g)~}$ were mixed with Cy5.5-PEG-COOH $\langle5\mathrm{mg}\rangle$ in ultrapure water (1 mL) under stirring at room temperature. 30 min later, above solution was purified via dialysis and lyophilized to result in Cy5.5-MOF QDs.   
Characterization. TEM and HR-TEM images were recorded on a FEITECNAI G220 high-resolution transmission electron microscope. Crystalline structures of samples were evaluated on a Rigaku-Dmax 2500 diffractometer. UV-vis absorbance measurements were carried out on a JASCO V-550 UV-vis spectrophotometer. Fluorescence measurements were carried out on a Jasco FP-6500 spectrofluorometer. $\zeta$ potential measurements were carried out on Malvern Nano ZS-90. Quantitative analysis of the $Z\mathbf{r}$ content was obtained by ICP-OES (Agilent 7700x series). FT-IR was obtained on a Bruker Vertex 70 FT-IR spectrometer, 32 scans were taken with a spectral resolution of $2\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Nitrogen sorption isotherm was measured by using a Micrometritics ASAP 2420 system with highpurity grade of gases at $77\mathrm{K}$ .   
$N_{2}$ adsorption-desorption measurement of PCN-224 NMOFs. Before measurement, PCN-224 NMOFs were washed with DMF for three times and acetone three times, respectively. Then, PCN-224 NMOFs were dried in a pre-heated $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ oven for more than $30\mathrm{min}$ . Finally, the resulting purple powder was activated on ASAP 2020 for $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption measurement at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{h}$ .   
TG analysis. For TG analysis, $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of PCN-224 NMOFs, PCN-224 QDs or MOF QDs was heated on a TGA-50 (Shimadzu) thermogravimetric system from room temperature to $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at a rate of 10 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ under $\Nu_{2}$ flow of $50\mathrm{mL}\mathrm{min^{-1}}$ .
Theoretical surface area of PCN-224. Theoretical surface area of PCN-224 were calculated by an Atom Volume & Surface calculation using the Materials Studio 8 software package with a solvent radius of $3.64\mathrm{~\AA~}(\mathrm{N}_{2})$ . The occupied volume, the free volume, as well as the surface area could be calculated as 11006.22 $\mathrm{\AA}^{3}$ , 45904.91 $\mathrm{\AA}^{3}$ , and $10227.07\mathrm{\AA}^{2}$ , respectively. Thus, theoretical surface area $({\mathrm m}^{2})=$ surface area $(\mathrm{\AA}^{2})/$ [density $(\mathbf{g}/\mathbf{cm}^{3})\times$ cell volume $(\mathrm{\AA}^{3})/10^{4}]$ , where cell volume $(\mathbb{A}^{3})=$ occupied volume $(\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3})+$ free volume $(\mathrm{\AA}^{3})$ . Thus, theoretical density of defect-free PCN-224 was $0.509\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{m}^{3}$ . Quantitative analysis of MOF QDs’ degradation. Standard solutions of TCPP were prepared in the mixture of $_\mathrm{H}\mathrm{_2O}$ and DMF with a volume ratio of 1:1. UV-vis spectra of TCPP standard solutions with various concentrations were measured. Calibration curve based on the absorbance at $416\mathrm{nm}$ and the concentration of TCPP was plotted. MOF QDs with a TCPP concentration of $25\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ dispersed in FBS, saline or ultrapure water were then dialyzed against these solutions, respectively. Related medium was taken out and measured on a Jasco-V550 UV-Vis spectrophotometer. Moreover, the amounts of Zr released from MOF QDs were quantified via ICP-OES. ROS generating ability of MOF QDs. DCFH-DA was used to explore the ROS generating ability of MOF QDs. MOF QDs with a TCPP concentration of $25\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ were mixed with DCFH-DA $({10}{\upmu\mathrm{M}})$ , and the solution was irradiated under a $650\ \mathrm{nm}$ laser $(1\mathrm{\Delta~W\cm^{-2}}$ ). After irradiation, fluorescence intensity at $525\mathrm{nm}$ was measured to explore the ROS generating ability $\left(\mathrm{Ex}\colon488~\mathrm{nm}\right)$ . $^{I}{\cal O}_{2}$ generating ability of MOF QDs. SOSG was used as a fluorescent probe to investigate the ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ generating ability of MOF QDs. In brief, SOSG ( $100~\upmu\mathrm{g})$ was dissolved in DMSO $(330\upmu\mathrm{L})$ to obtain SOSG stock solution $(0.5~\mathrm{mM})$ . Then, MOF QDs with a TCPP concentration of $25~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ were mixed with SOSG $(10~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ , and the solution was irradiated under a $650~\mathrm{nm}$ laser $(1\mathrm{\DeltaW\cm^{-2}})$ ). In the presence of ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ , SOSG exhibited a green fluorescence around $525\mathrm{nm}$ (Ex: 504 nm).
equation to obtain the correlation of fluorescence intensity $\mathrm{I_{F}}$ and irradiation time t:
$$
\mathrm{In}([\mathrm{SOSG}]/\mathrm{c}_{0})=\mathbf{-k}_{1}\mathrm{t}
$$
$$
\mathrm{I_{F}=A[1-e^{-k t}]}
$$
Where $\mathbf{c}_{0}$ was the initial concentration of SOSG, A and k were different fitting parameters. Thus:
$\mathbf{A}=\uprho_{\mathrm{f}}\mathrm{I}_{0}\upvarepsilon_{\mathrm{S}}\mathrm{bc}_{0}$ $\mathbf{k}=\boldsymbol{\Phi}_{\Delta}\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{ir}}\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_{\mathrm{PS}}\mathrm{bck}_{2}/\mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{d}}$
Where $\Phi_{\Delta}$ was the quantum yield of ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ generation, $\mathrm{\DeltaN_{ir}}$ was the irradiation light intensity by photons per second, $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{PS}}$ was the extinction coefficient of PS at the emission wavelength, as well as c was the concentration of PS. Linear approximations were used in above equations. By non-linear regression, we obtained a series of fit curves with the aforementioned function.   
Then, by deriving the equation $(\mathrm{I_{F}=A[1-e^{-k t}]})$ , we obtained:   
$\mathrm{I_{F}/d t}=\mathrm{Ake^{-kt}}$   
When $\mathrm{t}{\rightarrow}0$ ， $\mathrm{dI_{F}/d t}=\mathrm{Ak}$   
Thus, the product of Ak in the equation was proportional to the initial rate of the reaction that indicated the efficacies of ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ generation. Results based on all fitting parameters were provided in Table S1. Cell culture. Hela cells were cultured at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under $5\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Media was DMEM containing FBS $(10\%)$ , penicillin $(100\mathrm{U}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}.$ ), and streptomycin $(100\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL^{-1}})_{,}$ , which was changed every other day. Cells were digested by trypsin and re-suspended in fresh media before plating.   
MTT assay. Hela cells were seeded in a 96-well plate with a density of $10^{4}$ per well for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . MOF QDs with different concentrations were added into the medium. $24\mathrm{~h~}$ later, cells were treated with MTT for another $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ . DMSO was added to dissolve the formazan crystals, and Bio-Rad model-680
microplate reader was used to measure the absorbance at $490\mathrm{nm}$ with $570\mathrm{nm}$ as a reference. Cellular uptake. Hela cells were seeded in a 6-well plate with a density of $10^{5}$ per well. $24\mathrm{h}$ later, cells were treated with MOF QDs $25\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ of TCPP) for different periods. Then, cells were washed with $0.9\%$ NaCl solution and trypsinized. After centrifugation, cells were washed, re-suspended, and counted. Later, cells were dissolved in aqua regia at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{h}$ , and $Z\mathbf{r}$ amounts in above samples were explored via ICP-OES.
In vitro ROS generating ability of MOF QDs. Hela cells were seeded in a 6-well plate with a density of $10^{5}$ per well for 12 h. MOF QDs with a TCPP concentration of $25\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ were added and incubated for another $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . Then, cells were washed with saline and incubated with DCFH-DA ( $({10}~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ . 20 min later, cells were irradiated with a $650\ \mathrm{nm}$ laser ( $\textrm{l W}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ) for $10~\mathrm{min}$ . ROS fluorescence intensity of cells was monitored via flow cytometry and fluorescence microscopy.   
In vitro PDT efficacy. For in vitro PDT, Hela cells were seeded in a 96-well plate with a density of $10^{4}$ per well for $12\mathrm{h}$ . MOF QDs with a TCPP concentration of $25\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ were added and incubated for another $4\mathrm{h}$ . After cells were irradiated with a $650\mathrm{nm}$ laser $\mathrm{1\:W\thinspacecm^{-2}},$ ) for $10\mathrm{min}$ , cells were incubated for another $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . In vitro PDT efficacy was evaluated by MTT assay. Apoptosis amounts were determined by using Annexin V-FITC apoptosis detection kit. Briefly, cells were firstly treated with MOF QDs with a TCPP concentration of $25~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ in the presence or absence of a $650~\mathrm{nm}$ laser irradiation (1 W $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $10~\mathrm{min}\big)$ ). Then, cells were treated by trypsinization, washed with saline, resuspended in Annexin V binding buffer, incubated with Annexin V and PI at room temperature for 15 min in the dark, as well as subjected to flow cytometry.   
Cellular modality and viability observation. Hela cells were cultured in a 6-well plate with a density
of $10^{5}$ per well for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ to allow the attachment of cells. After cells were washed by saline, MOF QDs with a TCPP concentration of $25\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ were added to above medium. 6 h later, cells were irradiated with a $650~\mathrm{nm}$ laser $\mathrm{~(~1~W~cm^{-2}})$ ) for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ , and treated cells were placed in a incubator for further incubation. $24\mathrm{h}$ later, staining based on calcein AM and PI was used to confirm the visualized viability of cells.
Animal and tumor-bearing mouse model. All animal experiments were performed in accordance with the NIH guidelines for the care and use of laboratory animals (NIH Publication No. 85-23 Rev. 1985) and approved by the Jilin University Animal Care and Use Committee. Balb/c nude mice (8-10 week, $\mathrm{15~g)}$ were obtained from Laboratory Animal Centre of Jilin University (Changchun, China), and all animal care and handing procedures were in accordance with the guidelines approved by the ethics committee of Jilin University. Tumor-bearing mouse model was established at first. Hela cells were suspended in saline and subcutaneously injected on the back of mice. Then, tumor volume was calculated as (tumor length) $\times$ (tumor width)2 × 0.5.
Renal clearance analysis. Tumor-bearing mice were intravenously injected with MOF QDs with a TCPP concentration of $5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ . Afterwards, urine and feces were collected at different time points. Samples were dissolved in aqua regia at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ , and the $Z\mathrm{r}$ amounts were measured via ICPOES. TEM imaging was used to explore the urine samples.   
Pharmacokinetics. To explore the pharmacokinetics of MOF QDs and PEG-NMOFs, tumor-bearing mice were intravenously injected with MOF QDs or PEG-NMOFs with a TCPP concentration of $5\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}$ . Afterwards, blood was collected at different time points. Samples were incubated with aqua regia at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{h}$ , and the $Z\mathbf{r}$ amounts were measured via ICP-OES.   
Bio-distribution analysis. Tumor-bearing mice were intravenously injected with MOF QDs or PEGNMOFs with a TCPP concentration of $5~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{kg^{-1}}$ and sacrificed at different time points. Tumors and major organs were harvested for the quantitative analysis of the bio-distribution of MOF QDs or PEGNMOFs. Typically, tumors and major organs were surgically removed from the mice and dissolved in aqua regia. The mixtures were heated at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ , and the $Z\mathbf{r}$ amounts in above samples were explored via ICP-OES.
Fluorescence imaging of MOF QDs. Mice were intravenously injected with MOF QDs with a TCPP concentration of $5\mathrm{\mg\kg^{-1}(0.74\ m g\ k g^{-1}{\ C y5.5})}$ . Afterwards, mice were imaged at various expected time by using IVIS Spectrum 200. Moreover, mice were sacrificed and major organs and tumor were collected and imaged at various expected time points. Related images were analyzed using Image J Software.
In vivo PDT efficacy. Tumor-bearing mice were randomly divided into six groups and defined as control, light, MOF QDs, PEG-NMOFs, PEG-NMOFs $^+$ light, as well as MOF QDs $^+$ light, respectively. Then, mice were intravenously injected with MOF QDs or PEG-NMOFs with a TCPP concentration of $5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ twice a week. The groups of light, MOF QDs $^+$ light and PEG-NMOFs $+$ light received a $650\mathrm{nm}$ laser irradiation (1W $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $10\mathrm{min}\rangle$ ) at $12\mathrm{h}$ after intravenous injection. Tumor sizes were measured by using a caliper after various treatments. Tumor sizes were recorded for 3 weeks. Then, mice were sacrificed and tumors were collected for tumor mass measurement. Histological analysis. Tumor-bearing mice were randomly divided into six groups and defined as control, light, MOF QDs, PEG-NMOFs, PEG-NMOFs $^+$ light, as well as MOF QDs $^+$ light, respectively. Then, mice were intravenously injected with MOF QDs or PEG-NMOFs with a TCPP concentration of $5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ twice a week. The groups of light, MOF QDs $^+$ light and PEG-NMOFs $^+$ light received a $650\mathrm{nm}$ laser irradiation (1W $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $10\mathrm{min}\dot{}$ ) at $12\mathrm{{h}}$ after intravenous injection. 14 days later, mice were sacrificed, and major tissues including tumor, heart, liver, spleen, lung, and kidney were harvested and fixed with paraformaldehyde $(4\%)$ , dehydeated, embedded in paraffin, sectioned, and stained with H&E. Slides were observed on an Olympus BX-51 optical system. Immunohistochemical staining. Harvested tumors were fixed with paraformaldehyde $(4\%)$ , dehydeated, embedded in paraffin, sectioned, treated with TUNEL (colorimetric TUNEL apoptosis
assay kit, Beyotime), and observed on an Olympus BX-51 optical system.   
Survival rates analysis. Tumor-bearing mice were randomly divided into four groups and defined as control, light, MOF QDs, as well as MOF QDs $^+$ light, respectively. Mouse with a tumor greater than $20~\mathrm{mm}$ in any one dimension should be euthanized and be considered to be death for the purpose of drawing the survival curves. The survival rates of mice were recorded for 60 days.   
Body weight measurement. For body weight measurements, mice were separated into two groups. MOF QDs with a TCPP concentration of $5~\mathrm{mg~kg^{-1}}$ were intravenous injected into the mice, which were defined as the test. Mice without any treatment were defined as the control. Related mouse body weight was recorded for 30 days.
Hemolysis assay. Human blood samples stabilized by EDTA (1 mL) was added to saline solution (2 mL), and red blood cells were collected by centrifugation. Above samples were washed with saline, and purified red blood cells were diluted to 1/10 of its volume with saline. Diluted suspension (0.2 mL) was mixed with saline $(0.8\mathrm{mL}$ ) as negative control, ultrapure water $(0.8\mathrm{mL})$ ) as positive control, and MOF QDs suspensions $(0.8\mathrm{mL})$ ) as experimental groups. All the mixtures were vortexed and kept at room temperature for another $3\mathrm{~h~}$ . Then, above mixtures were centrifuged, and the absorbance of supernatants at $541~\mathrm{nm}$ was determined on an UV-vis spectroscopy. Hemolysis was calculated as the following formula: hemolysis percentage $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathrm{[(Abs_{sample}.}$ -Absnegative control)/(Abspositive control-Abs negative control)]×100%.
Hematology analysis and blood biochemical assay. Blood of mice treated with MOF QDs with a TCPP concentration of $5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ were collected at each expected time point after intravenous injection, and the samples were used to carry out hematological analysis and blood biochemical assay. Blood from the mice without any treatments were defined as the control.
Statistical analysis. All data were expressed as mean $\pm$ standard deviation (SD) and performed in 5 specimens. A p-value $<0.05$ was considered statistically significant. Statistical analysis was performed using one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) followed by post-hoc LSD test.
